  "readmeFile": "PACKAGE.md",
  "releaseNotes": "## v1.8.0 — Event-sink isolation, fail-closed verdicts, header-ownership guard\n\n**Hardening (tightening in a safe direction; the API surface stays additive):**\n- `SecurityEventDispatcher` isolates a throwing `ISecurityEventSink` per sink instead of muting every sink registered after it, and counts the swallowed failure in `SafeWebCoreMetrics.SecurityEventSinkFailures`. `FraudEventDispatcher` does the same in `SafeWebCoreFraudMetrics.FraudEventSinkFailures`\n- Fail-closed verdict mapping: an unrecognized verdict is now `RiskLevel.Unclassified` (appended, `= 4`) instead of `Low`, and the shared `FraudVerdictMapping` maps it to `RecommendedAction.BlockRequest` instead of `NoAction`\n- Startup validation now rejects an `AdditionalHeaders` entry that names a header SafeWebCore owns (HSTS, X-Frame-Options, CSP, NEL, Reporting-Endpoints, ...), because assigning that header replaced the library value — for CSP including the per-request nonce. Use `CustomPolicies` / `IHeaderPolicy` for a deliberate override\n\n**Also new:**\n- `SecurityEventDispatcher(IEnumerable<ISecurityEventSink>, SafeWebCoreMetrics?)` overload for the failure counter; the single-argument constructor keeps working\n- `SafeWebCore.FraudDetection` moves to the three-part version `1.1.0` (the four-part `1.0.0.0` normalizes to the already-published `1.0.0`)\n\n**Compatibility:** 100% backward compatible on the public surface — everything new is additive. Two intentional tightenings are documented in CHANGELOG.md: the `AdditionalHeaders` startup guard, and a consumer `switch` over `RiskLevel` without a default arm needing one arm for `Unclassified`.\n\nSee CHANGELOG.md and docs/ for full details.",
